</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Christos Ziakas
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>First and corresponding author. Correspondence to <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">c.ziakas24@imperial.ac.uk</span>.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Imperial College London
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nicholas Loo
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Imperial College London
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nishita Jain
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Imperial College London
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alessandra Russo
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Imperial College London
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Automated red-teaming has emerged as a scalable approach for auditing Large Language Models (LLMs) prior to deployment, yet existing approaches lack mechanisms to efficiently adapt to model-specific vulnerabilities at inference. We introduce Red-Bandit, a red-teaming framework that adapts online to identify and exploit model failure modes under distinct attack styles (e.g., manipulation, slang). Red-Bandit post-trains a set of parameter-efficient LoRA experts, each specialized for a particular attack style, using reinforcement learning that rewards the generation of unsafe prompts via a rule-based safety model. At inference, a multi-armed bandit policy dynamically selects among these attack-style experts based on the target model’s response safety, balancing exploration and exploitation. Red-Bandit achieves state-of-the-art results on AdvBench under sufficient exploration (ASR@10), while producing more human-readable prompts (lower perplexity). Moreover, Red-Bandit’s bandit policy serves as a diagnostic tool for uncovering model-specific vulnerabilities by indicating which attack styles most effectively elicit unsafe behaviors.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Warning: This paper includes content that may be offensive or harmful.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite significant advances in alignment to human values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Large Language Models (LLMs) remain vulnerable to adversarial prompts that bypass safety measures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. [5]</a></cite> attributes this vulnerability to competing objectives within LLMs and the generalization gap between safety and capabilities, indicating fundamental limitations of current architectures and training approaches. As LLMs are increasingly integrated with external tools and real-world applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, the breadth and severity of safety risks are expected to grow—from individual harms to large-scale societal impacts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>—highlighting the need for reliable auditing and red-teaming methods prior to deployment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing LLM evaluations often rely on human-annotated datasets to detect failure cases, such as toxicity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, hallucinations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and adversarial prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, but these datasets are limited in size and subject to selection biases. Human red-teaming, though effective, remains resource-intensive and limited in scalability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Therefore, automated red-teaming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> has emerged as a promising strategy, aiming to uncover unsafe outputs before deployment without requiring human oversight <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Adversarial prompt-optimization methods exploit privileged access, using gradients (white-box) or log-probabilities (gray-box), to craft jailbreaks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, with recent work improving readability and computational efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. However, while prompts from these methods transfer effectively to proprietary LLMs (black-box) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, they lack the ability to identify and exploit the target model’s vulnerabilities at test time. Iterative search and mutation methods refine candidate prompts online based on the target model’s responses, providing test-time adaptability but at the cost of being highly query-intensive <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. RL-based approaches post-train LLMs to efficiently generate human-readable adversarial prompts, but their diversity remains limited (mode collapse) due to the challenge of balancing multiple objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and diversity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> in reward design.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce Red-Bandit, a black-box red-teaming method that employs a multi-armed bandit to identify and exploit the most effective attack styles (e.g., role-playing, historical scenarios) for a given target model. Red-Bandit (i) trains a diverse set of LoRA attack-style experts using GRPO and reward on prompt-level safety, leveraging in-context conditioning to generate style-specific adversarial instructions, and (ii) dynamically selects among these attack-style experts at inference via a bandit policy guided by response-level safety. Red-Bandit leverages the efficiency of RL-trained LLM attackers while balancing exploration (stylistic diversity) and exploitation (attack success) at test time. Red-Bandit outperforms state-of-the-art approaches on the AdvBench dataset, achieving higher attack success rates and lower perplexity (human-readable prompts) under a sufficient exploration horizon. In addition, Red-Bandit provides a useful diagnostic tool for understanding model-specific vulnerabilities by revealing which attack styles each model is most susceptible to.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize our key contributions as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce Red-Bandit, a black-box red-teaming framework that employs a multi-armed bandit algorithm to identify effective attack styles at test time, dynamically balancing exploration (stylistic diversity) and exploitation (attack success).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an RL post-training pipeline that trains LoRA experts for each attack style using GRPO with prompt-level content safety rewards, leveraging in-context conditioning to generate human-readable and style-aligned adversarial prompts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that Red-Bandit outperforms state-of-the-art methods on AdvBench across both open-source and proprietary LLMs under a sufficient exploration horizon, while also serving as a diagnostic tool for identifying model-specific vulnerabilities across attack styles.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>RL Post-Training with GRPO</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> is widely used for RL post-training of LLMs due to its stability at scale <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. While effective, PPO is resource-intensive, requiring both a reference model for KL computation and training of a value model for advantage estimation, which significantly increases training overhead. Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> reduces this cost by eliminating the value model and estimating advantages via group-wise reward normalization. In our work, we adopt a variation of GRPO with LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> for parameter-efficient fine-tuning of attack-style experts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Multi-Armed Bandits</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The multi-armed bandit problem formalizes the exploration–exploitation trade-off in sequential decision-making under uncertainty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. At each step, an agent selects an action (arm) with an unknown reward distribution and receives stochastic feedback, aiming to maximize cumulative reward over time while balancing exploration of uncertain arms and exploitation of high-reward ones. The <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy algorithm implements this balance by selecting the empirically best arm with probability <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and a random arm with probability <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. The Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> instead applies optimism under uncertainty, selecting the arm with the highest upper confidence estimate of its expected reward. UCB favors exploration of less-explored arms, which receive wider confidence intervals, while exploiting well-sampled arms that yield higher empirical returns.
In our setting, Red-BANDIT instantiates the multi-arm bandit problem by treating each attack style (e.g., role play, slang, hypothetical) as an arm. The reward for choosing an arm is determined by the content safety of the target LLM’s response to a prompt generated in that style, as measured by a rule-based reward model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.07239v1/inference.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/237;" width="357" height="237"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Inference pipeline of Red-Bandit. At test time, a multi-armed bandit dynamically selects among attack-style LoRA experts based on response-safety reward from the target model, balancing exploration (stylistic diversity) and exploitation (attack success).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Automated Red-teaming</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a target LLM <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and a prompt space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, automated
red-teaming is formulated as a search for prompts that elicit harmful behavior.
Formally, we seek the prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}=\arg\max_{p\in\mathcal{P}}r\!\left(\mathcal{T}(p)\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.309em;" width="0.309em"><mi>r</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}=\arg\max_{p\in\mathcal{P}}r\!\left(\mathcal{T}(p)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\mathcal{T}(p))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\mathcal{T}(p))</annotation></semantics></math> is a scalar reward measuring the harmfulness of the
model’s response to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., computed via a rule-based verifier, a learned
classifier, or human judgment).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Post-Training of LoRA Experts</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We post-train LoRA-based attacker policies with reinforcement learning, using a
rule-based prompt-safety reward and style-conditioned prompts.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>RL Algorithm</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We post-train attacker policies using a reinforcement learning algorithm based on a variant of GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> (see Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 RL Post-Training with GRPO ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), which removes response-length and question-difficulty biases while retaining a group baseline for stable optimization. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rastogi et al. [30]</a></cite>, we omit the
KL penalty for efficiency. For samples <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\sim\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\sim\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, the clipped
policy-gradient loss is</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small\mathcal{L}_{\theta}=-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\theta}}\!\left[\min\!\left(r_{\theta}\hat{A},\,\text{clip}(r_{\theta},\,1-\epsilon,\,1+\epsilon)\,\hat{A}\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.673em;" width="1.673em"><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">clip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\mathcal{L}_{\theta}=-\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\theta}}\!\left[\min\!\left(r_{\theta}\hat{A},\,\text{clip}(r_{\theta},\,1-\epsilon,\,1+\epsilon)\,\hat{A}\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\theta}=\tfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(y|x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\theta}=\tfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(y|x)}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is the clipping threshold. The advantage estimate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small\hat{A}=R(x,y)-\tfrac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^{G}R(x,y_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">G</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">G</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small\hat{A}=R(x,y)-\tfrac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^{G}R(x,y_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> the rule-based reward and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> the number of responses per prompt.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Reward Function Design</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method relies on a rule-based reward function to identify unsafe content. In our formulation, the reward function is a binary <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> that scores prompts directly based on predefined rules, where a reward of 1 indicates the prompt x is unsafe. This approach allows training to proceed in a fully black-box setting without querying the target model, which substantially reduces computational cost and promotes attacker policies that generalize across downstream LLMs. For comparison, we also study alternative setups where the reward signal is derived from the target model’s response and from a combination of both prompt and response signals in a large-scale RL setup described in Section <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS2" title="4.3.2 Reward Design in RL Post-Training ‣ 4.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.07239v1/training.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/157;" width="405" height="157"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training pipeline of Red-Bandit. Multiple LoRA attack-style experts are trained with GRPO using rule-based rewards on prompt safety.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Attack-Style LoRA Experts</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement each attacker policy using LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, a parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) method that injects trainable low-rank adapters into frozen weight layers. Concretely, we train a separate LoRA expert for each of the 10 attack style categories defined in Rainbow Teaming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>: <em id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Slang, Technical Terms, Role Play, Authority Manipulation, Misspellings, Word Play, Emotional Manipulation, Hypotheticals, Historical Scenario,</em> and <em id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Uncommon Dialects</em>. Each expert is fine-tuned on prompts that explicitly include the attack style as an input token, leveraging in-context learning to generate style-specific adversarial instructions. Training follows the rule-based safety reward described previously, producing lightweight, style-conditioned attacker policies that can be adaptively selected at inference time. Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Examples of Attack Styles ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> provides examples of generated prompts given a behavior for each attack style.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Bandit-Guided Inference</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At inference, Red-Bandit treats each LoRA expert as an arm in a multi-armed bandit problem. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\{1,\dots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\{1,\dots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> arms,
corresponding to different attack styles. At round <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent selects an
arm <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and observes a reward <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{A_{t},t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{A_{t},t}</annotation></semantics></math> from the rule-based
model that scores the safety of the target LLM’s response. The
performance of a bandit policy is measured by its expected cumulative regret:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small R(T)=T\mu^{*}-\mathbb{E}\!\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}X_{A_{t},t}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">μ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.517em;" width="0.517em"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></munderover><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">A</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small R(T)=T\mu^{*}-\mathbb{E}\!\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}X_{A_{t},t}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{*}=\max_{i\in\mathcal{A}}\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{*}=\max_{i\in\mathcal{A}}\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean reward of the optimal arm. Our framework is agnostic to the choice of bandit algorithm. We evaluate the policy with UCB, chosen for its strong theoretical guarantees and robustness in balancing exploration and exploitation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, and with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy, which adopts a more exploitative strategy as described in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Multi-Armed Bandits ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Red-Bandit can be viewed as a post-hoc hard routing instantiation of a mixture-of-experts architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, where a test-time bandit policy performs discrete expert selection among pre-trained LoRA modules based on response feedback from the target model, instead of learning a router jointly during training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experiment Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>Implementation Details</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use Mistral-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> as the base model for prompt generation, selected for its minimal alignment fine-tuning and open-weight availability. Training follows the prompt template described in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Prompt Template for Attack-Style Generation ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. The reward model is instantiated with Llama Guard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, an instruction-tuned safeguard LLM that classifies prompts and responses into predefined safety risk levels and outputs binary safety decisions. We use Llama Guard-8B for RL training and Llama Guard-1B for bandit algorithms at inference time. Each attack-style expert is trained for one epoch using a GRPO variant, following the reinforcement learning pipeline described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Post-Training of LoRA Experts ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, with eight generations per step and LoRA adaptation. Full training, inference, and LoRA configurations, along with all hyperparameters, are detailed in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Model and Training Details ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2 </span>Evaluation Setup</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior work such as AdvPrompter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and Atoxia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, we use the AdvBench dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, which consists of 520 harmful instructions. We adopt the same 80/20 train–test split as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> to ensure fair comparability across methods. Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, we report the attack success rate (ASR), defined as the fraction of adversarial prompts that elicit harmful responses from the target LLM. We measure ASR under two sampling settings:
ASR@1, where only a single adversarial prompt is sampled per harmful instruction, and ASR@10, where ten adversarial prompts are sampled and a trial is counted as successful if at least one of them succeeds. Success is determined using keyword matching against a predefined list of refusal words, adopted by Atoxia and AdvBench, ensuring consistency and comparability with prior baselines. GPT-4o evaluations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> frequently misclassified successful attacks as non-toxic when the responses followed non-toxic stylistic patterns; therefore, we exclude these results, as detailed in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Limitations of GPT-4o Evaluations ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>. In addition, we evaluate prompt fluency using perplexity computed under our trained model. We report the average perplexity across generations, where lower values indicate more fluent and human-like text.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3 </span>Baselines</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, we compare against GCG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, AutoDAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, AdvPrompter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, and Atoxia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. For these baselines, we report the results directly from Atoxia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, where the hyperparameter settings are described. In addition, we evaluate Red-Bandit under an <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy policy (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>), which emphasizes exploitation, and our proposed UCB method with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, following standard practice in literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
