<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Open-Source Models (Gray-Box Setting)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report results on Mistral-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, Vicuna-7B (v1.5) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, and Llama2-7B-Chat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, evaluated using attack success rate (ASR) based on keyword matching and perplexity for prompt fluency (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1.3 Baselines ‣ 4.1 Experiment Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Baseline methods have gray-box access to the target model, whereas Red-Bandit adapts at test time without requiring such access. Across all three models, both Red-Bandit variants achieve the best performance in ASR@10 and perplexity, reaching <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> ASR@10 on Mistral-7B and Vicuna-7B. On Llama2-7B, both variants also outperform baselines, achieving ASR@10 above <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96\%</annotation></semantics></math>. The UCB variant attains the lowest perplexity in two of the three settings, generating more fluent and human-like prompts. For ASR@1, Red-Bandit outperforms Atoxia on Llama2-7B but not on Mistral-7B or Vicuna-7B. This outcome reflects the inherent exploration–exploitation trade-off in multi-armed bandit algorithms, where insufficient exploitation reduces attack success rates. Nevertheless, Red-Bandit consistently surpasses AdvPrompter, GCG, and AutoDAN across all models. The <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy variant, which prioritizes exploration, achieves higher ASR@1 than UCB on two of the three models, while UCB is more effective when multiple attempts are allowed, as reflected in the ASR@10 results</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Proprietary Models (Black-Box Setting)</h4>

