</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2.2 Proprietary Models (Black-Box Setting) ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports results on proprietary LLMs in the black-box setting: GPT-3.5-turbo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, GPT-4o-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, and GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. AdvPrompter and Atoxia rely on transferability, where prompts optimized for a source model are reused on the target. In contrast, Red-Bandit performs test-time adaptation to select the attack style directly on the target model. Red-Bandit achieves high ASR@10 across all targets (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="98.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>98.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">98.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> on GPT-3.5-turbo and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="93.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>93.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">93.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> on GPT-4o), demonstrating that a bandit algorithm operating over attack-style prompts can exploit vulnerabilities of closed-source LLMs without relying on transferability. Atoxia attains higher ASR@1 than Red-Bandit, as a single attempt per instruction favors exploration over exploitation. In contrast, Red-Bandit achieves higher ASR@10, highlighting the benefits of adaptive exploration in the black-box setting. Consistent with the open-source results, the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy variant, which prioritizes exploration, achieves higher ASR@1 than UCB on two of the three models, whereas UCB performs better when multiple attempts are available, as reflected in the ASR@10 results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3 </span>Identifying Attack-Style Vulnerabilities</h4>

<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Distribution of attack styles (%) for each bandit policy in the ASR@10 setting.
Style abbreviations: Historical Scenario (HS), Role Play (RP), Word Play (WP), Hypotheticals (H),
Uncommon Dialects (UD), Technical Terms (TT), Emotional Manipulation (EM), Slang (S),
Authority Manipulation (AM), and Misspellings (M).
Bold and underlined values denote the most and second-most frequent styles, respectively.
</span></figcaption>
