<th id="S4.T4.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Variant</span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">ASR@1 (Keyword) <math id="S4.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.6.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><math id="S4.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\text{norm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">H</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps" mathsize="0.900em">norm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\text{norm}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T4.6.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;"> <math id="S4.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.6.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">PPL <math id="S4.T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T4.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;" rowspan="4"><span id="S4.T4.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Llama3.1-8B</span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.6.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Baseline (no RL, no Bandit)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T4.6.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">38.5</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">0.98</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">2.45</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.6.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.6.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Red-Bandit (no RL)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T4.6.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">50.9</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">0.65</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">2.22</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.6.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.6.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Red-Bandit (no Bandit)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T4.6.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">55.8</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">0.98</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">2.65</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.6.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.6.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Red-Bandit (RL + Bandit)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T4.6.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">58.7</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">0.67</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:-0.25pt;padding-bottom:-0.25pt;"><span id="S4.T4.6.5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">2.62</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct an ablation study to isolate the contributions of RL post-training and the multi-armed bandit to overall performance. Four attacker variants are evaluated against a Llama-3.1-8B target following the setup described in the previously: (i) <em id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Baseline</em> (no RL, no bandit), which conditions the LLM on the attack style in the prompt and samples styles uniformly at test time;; (ii) <em id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Red-Bandit (no RL)</em>, which applies bandit-based selection of attack styles at test time using the base LLM; (iii) <em id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Red-Bandit (no bandit)</em>, which employs RL fine-tuning implemented via LoRA during training but samples attack styles uniformly; and (iv) <em id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Red-Bandit (RL + bandit)</em>, our full method using an <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy policy. We report ASR@1 (keyword matching), normalized style entropy <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\text{norm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mtext>norm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\text{norm}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and perplexity. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3.1 Impact of RL Post-Training and Bandits ‣ 4.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, both RL post-training and the bandit component contribute substantially to overall performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2 </span>Reward Design in RL Post-Training</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate alternative reward formulations to assess their impact on large-scale RL post-training for LLM prompt generators in attack style, following the DeepSeek-R1 training recipe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. In addition to our proposed prompt-based reward, we consider response-based and hybrid reward variants. Prompt-only reward yields more efficient and diverse training, outperforming the other designs in terms of training time and VRAM utilization. It also achieves higher ASR@20 on the HarmBench benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> against Llama-3.1-8B and Qwen3-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, while performing on par with hybrid training. We provide the full analysis in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Impact of Reward Design in Large-Scale RL Post-Training ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Prompt Optimization Methods</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adversarial prompt-optimization methods leverage privileged model access to craft effective jailbreaks, using gradients (white-box) or log-probabilities (gray-box). GCG (Greedy Coordinate Gradient) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> optimizes adversarial suffixes directly via gradient information from the target model, achieving high attack success rates but producing low-readable prompts. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jones et al. [43]</a></cite> similarly explores automated prompt search for eliciting toxic outputs, but their approach is limited by the computational cost of discrete optimization. To address these issues, recent gray-box methods focus on improving human readability and efficiency. AdvPrompter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> trains an auxiliary LLM through alternating optimization to generate coherent, human-readable suffixes, while Atoxia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> employs reinforcement learning to synthesize adversarial queries and openings conditioned on a target harmful response. Although both methods achieve strong transferability to black-box models, their attack policies are trained offline, preventing them from adapting to a target model’s vulnerabilities at test time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Iterative and Search-Based Methods</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Black-box iterative methods refine adversarial prompts at test time by conditioning on the target model’s responses. PAIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> performs feedback-driven refinement through attacker–target interaction, while TAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> employs tree search with pruning to discard suboptimal candidates. GAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> generalizes this paradigm using graph-structured search to maintain global context across attack trajectories. AutoDAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> and GPTFuzzer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> apply genetic and fuzzing-inspired mutations to evolve semantically coherent adversarial prompts, whereas CipherChat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> leverages ciphered or non-natural text to bypass safety filters. Rainbow Teaming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> frames adversarial prompt generation as a quality–diversity optimization problem, performing open-ended search using MAP-Elites to populate an archive of prompts spanning diverse attack styles and risk categories. While effective, these methods are computationally expensive at inference, requiring many queries to generate and evaluate candidate prompts for a single successful attack. In contrast, Red-Bandit enables efficient test-time adaptation through online selection among pre-trained attack-style experts as the bandit’s exploration–exploitation trade-off stabilizes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>RL-Based Methods</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RL-based approaches post-train LLMs to generate human-readable adversarial prompts but are prone to mode collapse, limiting prompt diversity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. To mitigate this, recent work introduces curiosity-driven exploration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, GFlowNet fine-tuning with MLE smoothing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, and multi-step RL with auto-generated rule-based rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. While these methods improve diversity, they remain constrained by the inherent difficulty of modeling multiple objectives and stylistic variation in reward design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Moreover, prior RL-based approaches do not adapt their attack strategy to specific target models at test time. Red-Bandit overcomes this limitation by introducing diversity through multiple style-specific LoRA experts and enabling model-specific adaptation via a test-time bandit policy guided by response feedback. SoC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> proposed a multi-armed bandit framework for generating jailbreak attacks, where the bandit learns to select context-switching query categories that progressively weaken model safeguards. In contrast, Red-Bandit performs test-time adaptation at the attack-style level, selecting among pre-trained LoRA experts trained via reinforcement learning. Beyond adaptation, we interpret the learned bandit policy as a diagnostic tool that identifies model-specific vulnerabilities to particular attack styles.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion, Limitations, and Future Work</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Discussion</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that exploiting model vulnerabilities through targeted attack styles is highly effective, outperforming existing methods in both attack success rate given sufficient exploration and human-readability. Efficient RL post-training with LoRA further improves attack effectiveness over in-context conditioning by enabling style-aligned prompt generation. Beyond enhancing attack performance, the multi-armed bandit also improves interpretability: by analyzing the distribution of selected attack styles, Red-Bandit exposes model-specific weaknesses and behavioral biases, offering insights into how different LLMs respond to distinct adversarial framings. Beyond enhancing attack performance, Red-Bandit can be used as a diagnostic tool: by analyzing the distribution of selected attack styles, it reveals model-specific weaknesses and behavioral biases, offering insights into how different LLMs respond to distinct attack style patterns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Limitations</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach introduces additional computational overhead during inference compared to transferable prompt-based methods, as it requires access to a rule-based reward model (e.g., Llama Guard 1B). While post-training remains efficient due to parameter-efficient fine-tuning, overall training complexity still scales with the number of attack styles, since each style-specific expert must be post-trained separately. Finally, extending the framework to custom datasets or behavioral domains may be challenging when LLM-based prompt generators cannot reliably condition on attack-style instructions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Future Work</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Red-Bandit’s bandit-based test-time adaptation framework is method-agnostic and readily extends beyond LoRA-based experts to other LLM red-teaming approaches, including even human red-teamers. The framework can also be used to discover and exploit model vulnerabilities tied to specific attack primitives, not merely attack styles. A further direction is to compose multiple attack styles within a single attempt or to leverage partial gray-box access to improve adaptability and performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Potential Risks and Societal Impact</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the Red-Bandit framework is designed to advance LLM safety, we acknowledge its dual-use nature. The same techniques used to identify safety vulnerabilities could, in principle, be misused by malicious actors to generate harmful content or develop more effective jailbreaks. The adversarial prompts produced by our method are intended solely for developers and researchers to audit and strengthen model safeguards.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work was supported by UKRI (EP/Y037111/1) as part of the ProSafe project (EU Horizon 2020, MSCA, grant no. 101119358). We thank Prof. Yves-Alexandre de Montjoye and Prof. Marek Rei for their valuable feedback.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
