<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Impact of Reward Design in Large-Scale RL Post-Training</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the large-scale RL training recipe of DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, we initialize the LLM prompt generator from a base model via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on red-teaming exemplars that pair harmful behaviors with concrete attack prompts. Specifically, we use 80,000 prompts from the extended open-source HarmBench corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> to reduce refusal rates and teach the mapping from behavior descriptions to adversarial prompt templates, substantially improving stability for subsequent RL fine-tuning. Following SFT, we post-train the LLM attacker with a variant of GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> on AdvBench-v4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, extending AdvBench to 4,948 distinct harmful behaviors. Rewards are provided by our rule-based reward model (<span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama Guard</span>-8B) and applied in three configurations: prompt-only (rewarding the attacker’s prompts), response-only (rewarding the target model’s responses), and hybrid (an equal-weight combination of both). This setup enables controlled ablations over reward design.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation follows the HarmBench benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> on 200 diverse harmful behaviors. For each behavior, the attacker generates up to 20 independent adversarial prompts, each submitted separately to the target model without conversational carry-over. A behavior is considered successfully attacked if any attempt elicits a harmful response, where harmfulness is determined by the HarmBench pretrained classifier applied to the target model’s outputs. This yields the Attack Success Rate (ASR@20). Our SFT and RL corpora partially overlap with the HarmBench evaluation set; while this may inflate absolute ASR values, the overlap is consistent across all training configurations, ensuring that relative comparisons between reward functions remain fair. To assess diversity, we compute Self-BLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> across 20 generations per behavior, where lower scores indicate more diverse attack strategies. We also record VRAM usage and wall-clock training time to compare the efficiency of different reward configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix A Examples of Attack Styles ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows that prompt-based rewards provide the most effective and scalable training signal. They achieve the highest attack success on Qwen-8B (82.0%), the best diversity (lowest Self-BLEU), and substantially lower computational cost compared to response-based or hybrid setups. While hybrid rewards yield a minor gain on Llama-3.1-8B, they require more resources and reduce diversity, making prompt-based feedback the preferable choice for large-scale adversarial training.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix A Examples of Attack Styles ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> indicates that prompt-based rewards provide the most effective and scalable training signal. This configuration achieves the highest attack success on Qwen-8B (82.0%), the lowest Self-BLEU (indicating greater diversity), and substantially lower computational cost compared to response-based or hybrid objectives. Although hybrid rewards yield marginal gains on Llama-3.1-8B, they incur higher resource usage and reduced diversity. Overall, prompt-level supervision emerges as the most effective objective for large-scale adversarial training, balancing performance, diversity, and efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Model and Training Details</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use Mistral-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> as the base model for prompt generation, chosen for its minimal alignment fine-tuning and open-weight availability. The reward model is instantiated with Llama Guard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, an instruction-tuned safeguard LLM that classifies prompts and responses into predefined safety risk levels and outputs binary safety decisions. We use Llama Guard-8B for RL training and Llama Guard-1B for bandit algorithms at inference time. Table <a href="#A3.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix C Model and Training Details ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> lists the hyperparameters used for RL and LoRA training, along with the inference configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For RL training, we conduct a sweep over <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{epochs}\in\{1.0,3.0,5.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>epochs</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{epochs}\in\{1.0,3.0,5.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{gradient accumulation steps}\in\{1,2,4,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>gradient accumulation steps</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{gradient accumulation steps}\in\{1,2,4,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{learning rate}\in\{1\times 10^{-7},1\times 10^{-6}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>learning rate</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{learning rate}\in\{1\times 10^{-7},1\times 10^{-6}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our RL algorithm eliminates the need for a reference model, value network, and KL regularization as discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Post-Training of LoRA Experts ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. For LoRA, we explore <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{r}\in\{4,8,16\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>r</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{r}\in\{4,8,16\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{LORA\_ALPHA}\in\{16,32,64\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>LORA_ALPHA</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{LORA\_ALPHA}\in\{16,32,64\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the bandit algorithm, we set the <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy exploration rate to <math id="A3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and the UCB exploration constant to <math id="A3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The final configuration is selected based on training stability and performance in large-scale RL experiments provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Impact of Reward Design in Large-Scale RL Post-Training ‣ Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. We use the AdamW optimizer with <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, and a linear learning rate with no warmup. The same optimizer configuration is applied across all LoRA fine-tuning attack styles. All training and inference are performed in bfloat16 mixed precision on a single NVIDIA A100 (80 GB).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A3.T7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 7</span>: </span><span id="A3.T7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
